      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent starting point, but \"long-term maintenance and accessibility\" is still a bit too generic. If a reporter at the Chronicle asks, \"Which specific section of the bill failed to address maintenance?\" or \"What specific accessibility standard was violated?\", they're going to tear us apart if we just give them vague platitudes.\n\nWe need to give this actual teeth. I need a specific, technical \"smoking gun\" we can point to. For example, we could say the bill failed to comply with a specific state-level building code requirement (like a specific ADA subset or a local infrastructure mandate), or that the revenue projections in Section 3 lacked a mandatory contingency buffer required by city ordinance. \n\nGive me a concrete, authoritative detail—a specific clause, a specific code, or a specific percentage error—that we can use as our anchor. What's the specific \"technical catch\" he caught that makes him look like a watchdog instead of just an opponent?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
